[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Optimization in .NET: Span<T>, Memory<T>, ArrayPool, and stackalloc"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"High-performance .NET applications require careful management of allocations to minimize Garbage Collection (GC) pressure. Traditional approaches using arrays and strings often lead to excessive heap allocations. This tutorial explores advanced memory management types introduced in modern .NET to write zero-allocation or low-allocation code."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding stackalloc"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The stackalloc keyword allows you to allocate a block of memory on the stack rather than the heap. Stack-allocated memory is automatically freed when the method returns, bypassing the Garbage Collector entirely. This is ideal for small, short-lived buffers."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"Span<byte> buffer = stackalloc byte[1024];
// Process the stack-allocated buffer
ProcessData(buffer);"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Span<T> and ReadOnlySpan<T>"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Span<T> is a ref struct that provides a type-safe and memory-safe representation of contiguous regions of arbitrary memory. Because it is a ref struct, it can only reside on the stack, which prevents it from escaping to the heap (e.g., in async methods)."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"int[] array = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
Span<int> span = array.AsSpan(1, 3);
// span now represents elements { 2, 3, 4 }
span[0] = 99; // Modifies the original array"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Memory<T> and ReadOnlyMemory<T>"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike Span<T>, Memory<T> is not a ref struct and can be placed on the heap. This makes Memory<T> suitable for scenarios where you need to pass data across asynchronous boundaries, where ref structs are prohibited."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public async Task ProcessDataAsync(Memory<byte> memory)
{
    await Task.Delay(100);
    // Slice the memory safely
    Memory<byte> slice = memory.Slice(0, 10);
    Process(slice.Span);
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"ArrayPool<T>"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you need large buffers that cannot fit on the stack, allocating new arrays frequently causes Gen 0/Gen 1 GC pressure. ArrayPool<T>.Shared provides a resource pool that rents and returns arrays, reducing object allocation overhead."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"int bufferSize = 4096;
byte[] poolArray = ArrayPool<byte>.Shared.Rent(bufferSize);
try
{
    Span<byte> poolSpan = poolArray.AsSpan(0, bufferSize);
    // Perform high-performance operations
}
finally
{
    ArrayPool<byte>.Shared.Return(poolArray);
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use stackalloc only for small buffers (typically under 256-1024 bytes) to avoid StackOverflowException."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer Span<T> for synchronous, high-throughput data processing algorithms."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Memory<T> when working with asynchronous code or storing representations inside classes."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always return rented arrays from ArrayPool<T> inside a try-finally block to prevent memory leaks."}]
